.TH PROGRAMMERS\-SCREENSHOT 1 "2026-08-02" "programmers-screenshot 0.18.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
programmers\-screenshot \- region screenshot tool for hotkey binding
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B programmers\-screenshot
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Freezes the screen and dims it, then puts a toolbar across the top of every
monitor. Mark out a region, draw on it, and press
.B Capture
in the top right corner. The result is copied to the clipboard and written as
a timestamped PNG to
.IR ~/Pictures/Screenshots ,
whose path is printed to standard output.
.PP
Nothing is captured until you say so, so a selection can be redrawn as many
times as you like first.
.SH SOUND
A shutter sound plays as the shot is taken, using the first of
.BR canberra\-gtk\-play (1),
.BR paplay (1),
.BR pw\-play (1)
or
.BR aplay (1)
that is installed. With none of them the capture is silent and nothing else
changes. The desktop's own event-sounds setting is honoured, and
.B \-\-no\-sound
suppresses it for one invocation.
.SH NOTIFICATION
The notification shown afterwards carries two buttons:
.TP
.B Open Image
Opens the PNG in whatever handles images.
.TP
.B Show in Folder
Opens the containing folder with the file selected, via the
.B org.freedesktop.FileManager1
interface. Without a file manager on the bus it falls back to opening the
folder itself.
.PP
GNOME collapses notifications that carry buttons, so the two may sit behind
the notification's expander arrow rather than being visible immediately.
.PP
Buttons need a process alive to receive the click, but the command itself
exits at once so shell pipelines do not block. The notification is therefore
owned by a short-lived detached helper, which exits when the notification is
dismissed, when a button is pressed, or after five minutes.
.SH TOOLS
.TP
.B Region
Click and drag to mark out what will be captured. Starting a new drag deletes
the region already there, and a single click clears it. The region is optional: with none marked out, Capture
takes the whole screen.
.TP
.B Pen
Draw freehand on the frozen screen, in the colour and thickness chosen on the
settings row. Annotations are baked into the captured image.
.TP
.B Highlighter
Freehand, like the pen, but laid down as a translucent wash in its own marker
colours. It tints what is underneath rather than covering it, so text stays
readable. Translucent rather than multiplied: multiply is what a highlighter
does on paper and looks better on a light screenshot, but it barely touches a
dark one, and most screenshots are dark.
.TP
.B Line
Drag out a straight line, a rectangle, an outlined circle, or an arrow,
whichever is chosen on the settings row. The rectangle and the circle are
outlines, so neither hides what it is drawn around.
.TP
.B Measure
Drag between two points to read the distance. The reading is in physical
pixels, which on a HiDPI screen is not the same as the logical ones the drag
covers. A drag along an axis reads as a single number; anything else shows
width, height and the diagonal. Holding Shift snaps to 45 degrees, which is
how you get an exactly horizontal or vertical reading. The measurement is
committed like any other annotation, so it ends up in the capture.
.TP
.B Colour picker
Click a pixel and its colour goes on the clipboard, as
.B #RRGGBB
or as
.BR rgb() ,
whichever the settings row says. A swatch and the value are shown where you
clicked so you can see that it worked. Nothing is added to the drawing, so
there is nothing to undo and nothing of it in the capture. Capturing puts the
image on the clipboard and so replaces the colour: press Escape instead if the
colour is what you came for.
.TP
.B Redact
Drag out a bar that covers something completely, in black or white. The fill
replaces the pixels underneath, so nothing of the original survives into the
saved PNG. This matters: pixelating or blurring a secret only averages the
pixels, and those averages leak \- pixelated text can be recovered by
rendering candidate strings, pixelating them the same way and matching. Use
this for tokens, keys and addresses.
.TP
.B Pixelate
Drag out an area and it is broken into coarse blocks, at one of three sizes.
The blocks are averaged from the frozen screen when the drag ends. This is for
faces and clutter, not for secrets: the averages leak, and pixelated text can
be recovered by rendering candidate strings, pixelating them the same way and
matching. Use
.B Redact
for anything that must not get out.
.TP
.B Step
Click to drop a numbered badge, counting 1, 2, 3 and on as you go, for marking
out the steps of a process. Drag before releasing to place one exactly.
Undoing a badge hands its number back, so the next one takes it again. The
numeral is drawn light or dark, whichever reads against the badge colour.
.TP
.B Text
Click to place a caret and type. Enter starts a new line, Backspace deletes,
and clicking away commits the text. Switching tools or capturing commits it
too, so nothing typed is lost. With
.B Backing
turned on the text sits on a filled white box, one box behind the whole
paragraph, as wide as the longest line and as tall as all of them together.
The box is always white; only the text takes the chosen colour.
.PP
Holding
.B Shift
while dragging constrains it: the region, the rectangle and the circle go
square, and lines and arrows snap to 45 degree angles.
.PP
A tool that has settings gets a second toolbar row beneath the first, holding
its own options. Setting values are shared between tools, so a colour chosen
for one is the colour the next one uses.
.PP
Hovering any button on either row names it, which is the only place the
colour swatches and the line tool's shape icons are labelled.
.SH INTERFACE
.TP
.B Capture
Top right. Always available.
.TP
.B ✕
Left of Capture. Closes without capturing.
.TP
.B Sliders
Left of ✕. Opens the settings window, which chooses whether captures are
saved to a folder and which folder that is. What it stores is a default: an
explicit
.B \-\-directory
or
.B \-\-no\-save
on the command line still wins.
.TP
.B Enter
Same as pressing Capture.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z ", " Ctrl+Shift+Z
Undo and redo. Setting the region is undoable just as drawing is.
.TP
.B Escape ", right click"
Cancel. Nothing is written and the exit status is 1. Pressing Escape during a
drag abandons only that stroke, and while typing it discards only the text.
.PP
The toolbar is not a drawing surface, so a drag cannot be *started* underneath
it. Dragging upwards from below into the toolbar strip does still work, which
is how you capture the top edge of the screen.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-full
Capture the entire screen immediately, skipping the overlay entirely.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the PNG to \fIFILE\fR instead of a timestamped name.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-directory " " \fIDIR\fR
Save into \fIDIR\fR instead of the default directory, overriding the folder
chosen in the settings window.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-clipboard
Do not touch the clipboard.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-save
Do not write a file; copy to the clipboard only. The settings window can
make this the default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sound
Do not play the shutter sound.
.TP
.BR \-\-install\-hotkey " [" \fIACCEL\fR ]
Register a GNOME custom shortcut that runs this command. \fIACCEL\fR is a
GTK accelerator string and defaults to \fBPrint\fR.
.IP
GNOME holds Print for its own screenshot UI, and a custom shortcut does not
reliably win while it does, so this switches the built-in off and reports
what it took. The old value is written to
.I ~/.config/programmers-screenshot/preferences.json
What the settings window stores.
.TP
.I ~/.config/programmers-screenshot/displaced.json
and
.B \-\-uninstall\-hotkey
puts it back. An accelerator nobody holds disturbs nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall\-hotkey
Remove the shortcut registered by \fB\-\-install\-hotkey\fR, and restore any
GNOME binding it displaced.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A screenshot was captured and delivered.
.TP
.B 1
The capture was cancelled, or the screen could not be read.
.TP
.B 2
The options given were contradictory.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Pictures/Screenshots
Default output directory, created on first use.
.TP
.I ~/.config/programmers-screenshot/preferences.json
What the settings window stores.
.TP
.I ~/.config/programmers-screenshot/displaced.json
GNOME shortcuts switched off to free the hotkey, kept so they can be restored.
.SH NOTES
Under X11 the screen is captured directly from the root window. Under Wayland
it goes through the
.B org.gnome.Shell.Screenshot
D\-Bus interface, so a GNOME session is required there.
.PP
The overlay spans every monitor, so a selection may cross screen boundaries.
Every monitor gets its own copy of the toolbar, laid out to that screen's
width. They are all views of the same state, so a tool or a colour chosen on
one applies everywhere. The toolbar strip cannot start a drag on any screen,
though dragging upward into it still works.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xclip (1),
.BR wl\-copy (1)
